# Writing

Write like a WIRED feature, a New York Times explainer, or a piece in The Information. Complete sentences. Reported prose. A curious adult explaining something real.

## One reader, three depths

A topic page is one article. The same person reads all three sections, and each section goes deeper than the last.

1. Under the title: one everyday picture. What the thing is. Stop there. Do not preview the mechanism or the history.
2. The concept: how it works. New information. Assume they already have the picture.
3. The industry section: who did what, when, what broke when it shipped. Assume they already have the mechanism.

Do not recap. Do not write the same idea at three altitudes for three imagined audiences.

## Do

- Subject, verb, object. If you are listing kinds of a thing, fold them into one sentence with "for some" / "for others" / "including."
- Ordinary words. Unpack terms. Keep people, years, and jobs.
- Every sentence has to follow from the one before it. A stack of true-sounding lines is not an explanation.

## Do not

- Fragment lists pretending to be paragraphs: "Match a typical person. Match an expert. Keep learning."
- Forced contrast punchlines: "It is X. It is Y."
- Cheap words: trick, clever, dangerous, trophy, slogan, theater.
- Meta: "Say this in a talk."
- On AGI in particular: section 1 still explains the idea, once, the way Data explains a pile of examples. Later sections do not redefine it, and do not recast it as a task list. They go into how people live with the almost-version, why that is exciting and hard at once, and what named exams and labs are actually doing.

## Example

Bad: "Match a typical person on most desk jobs. Match an expert. Keep learning after you leave the factory."

Good: "For some researchers, it means a system that can handle most desk jobs about as well as a typical adult. For others, it means expert-level work, or a system that keeps learning after it leaves the lab."
